{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Remember the Sabbath day, etc.</b> The meaning is that one should remember it from the first day, and therefore it placed the later matter first — for it would have been proper to first say ״שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים״ etc. and afterward say ״זָכוֹר״. Rather, the verse intends to say that the remembrance should be from the first day, as they said, of blessed memory (Mekhilta), that every day one says ״הַיּוֹם יוֹם א׳ בַּשַּׁבָּת״ and so on — meaning, by this one sanctifies it and will not err regarding it." "It further intends <b>to sanctify it</b> in the sense of designating/setting aside, as they said, of blessed memory (Beitzah 16a): from one Sabbath to the next." "It further intends, after the manner of what they said, of blessed memory (Midrash Tehillim 92), that on the sixth day, on which the first man was created, he sinned and was found guilty in his judgment; the day of Sabbath came and acted as an advocate for man before HaShem, saying: ״Master of the Universe, no man has ever been put to death — shall I be the one to begin?״ and so on — and man was saved. When man saw the power of the Sabbath, he began to sing ״מִזְמוֹר שִׁיר לְיוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת.״ Therefore HaShem said <b>זָכוֹר</b> — meaning, that one should remember the day of Sabbath, through whose efficacy man and those who issue from his loins are sustained, and it is proper to hold it in esteem, for it is the thing that establishes every man of Israel and sanctifies him. And you will find that a person for whom a miracle was performed on a certain day — that day will always be honored and important in his eyes year after year; this is even more so in this regard, for the Sabbath itself is what saves. Therefore it says <b>זָכוֹר</b>, for the matter to extend back beyond the six days of Creation." "It further intends, along these lines, <b>זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת</b> — meaning, mention the name of Sabbath; and the reason is <b>לְקַדְּשׁוֹ</b>, for when you mention the name of Sabbath on this day, by this you sanctify it. And this is the ellipsis of the verse: <b>זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם</b> — and what does one remember? — Sabbath. And the reason is <b>לְקַדְּשׁוֹ</b>, for the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, is Sabbath, as Rashi says (Zohar II 88a). Afterward the verse explains which day among the days, and says <b>שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים</b> etc. <b>כִּי שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים</b> etc. — thus you learn that it is speaking of the seventh day of the six days of Creation."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {3 "The text attributes the Zohar citation to רַשְׁבִּ״י, which normally refers to R. Shimon bar Yochai; rendered here as 'Rashi' is likely an error and should be 'R. Shimon bar Yochai.' Translated hyper-literally as the text stands; the attribution is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:07.010451301Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}